{"study_accession":"PRJNA1429817","secondary_study_accession":"SRP679987","title":"Assessing the impact of parental linear gene normalization in the performance of statistical models for circular RNA differential expression analysis","description":"Circular RNAs (circRNAs) have emerged as promising non-invasive cancer biomarkers due to their exceptional stability, abundance in body fluids, and diverse regulatory roles. However, the identification of differentially expressed circRNAs remains challenging, partly because filtering and normalization strategies are not standardized. While most analyses rely on circular read counts alone, newer approaches incorporate parental gene counts as additional normalization factors. To date, no systematic benchmarking has been performed to determine how these normalization strategies influence differential expression results that limits reproducibility and constrains clinical translation.</p><p>In this study, we evaluated the impact of multiple normalization approaches on circRNA differential expression analysis using five real datasets, of which three were retrieved from online studies and two were collected in-house from blood platelet samples. An addition, 1140 semi-parametric in silico datasets were also analyzed. We benchmarked three widely used statistical frameworks, limma-voom, DESeq2, and edgeR, selected following a comprehensive survey of circRNA bioinformatics literature. Performance was assessed across a range of evaluation metrics to determine the robustness and reliability of each normalization strategy.","pubmed":"","geo_series":"","n_samples":19,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN56255797","study_accession":"PRJNA1429817","blood_class":"platelet","class_from":"sample","title":"Cancer","description":"Cancer","sex":"female","geo_sample":"","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2026-06-27","attributes":[["isolate","platelet"],["age","67"],["biomaterial_provider","Bekes County Central Hospital Pandy Ka